<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Ghaghra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1529">1529</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Battle of the Ghaghra, Battle of Gogra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Ghaghra was fought on 6 May 1529 near the confluence of the Ghaghra and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> defeated the Afghan chiefs of eastern India and the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasiruddin-nusrat-shah">Nasiruddin Nusrat Shah</a>, sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, who had supported them. It was Babur's last major battle and was followed by a peace treaty with Bengal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ghaghra.jpg" alt="Battle of Ghaghra" width="320" height="143" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pardeepsingh2657 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Ghagra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-panipat">First Battle of Panipat</a> in 1526 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-khanwa">Battle of Khanwa</a> in 1527. After Khanwa, Sultan Mahmud Lodi, brother of the late Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a> and claimant to the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. Bihar meanwhile was torn by rival factions under its young ruler Sultan Jalal ud-Din Lohani, whose affairs were managed by his mother Dudu and by Farid Khan, later known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaunpur">Jaunpur</a> invited Mahmud to take the throne of Bihar and Jaunpur, and he soon held nearly all of Bihar.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> set out from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> on 2 February 1529 and reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> at Dakdaki on 27 February, where his son Askari and other generals joined him. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a>, some 100,000 strong, had gathered round Mahmud, but as Babur advanced they lost their nerve: Mahmud raised his siege of Chunar, and Sher Shah abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Benares</a>. Near Ghazipur, Jalal ud-Din Lohani, Sher Shah and other Afghan chiefs sent their submission, and Mahmud took refuge with the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasiruddin-nusrat-shah">Nusrat Shah</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> at the junction of the Ghaghra and the Ganges.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> army held a strong position with 100 to 150 boats, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>, judging that it had broken its neutrality, sent an envoy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasiruddin-nusrat-shah">Nusrat Shah</a>. With no satisfactory reply, he formed six divisions. Four, under Askari and Sultan Junaid Barlas, were to cross the Ghaghra upstream at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haldi">Haldi</a> and draw the enemy out, while his chief artillery commander, Ustad Ali Quli, and the engineer Mustafa Rumi bombarded the Bengali camp from across the rivers.</p><p>On the morning of 6 May, as Askari's army moved, the Bengali troops advanced to meet it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> ordered his own division and Muhammad Zaman Mirza's across the river, some in boats, some swimming, some floating on reeds. They met stiff resistance on landing, but the Bengali army, surrounded on three sides, left the field in confusion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> gave up hope of an Afghan government in the east and submitted, and Jalal ud-Din Lohani came with his chief <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">amirs</a> to acknowledge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a>. Nusrat Shah accepted the peace terms Babur's envoy had offered. Babur spent his remaining months consolidating his empire and died on 26 December 1530, succeeded by his eldest son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Ghaghra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of the Mughals and Afghans in the north brought harder enemies: Babur defeated a Bengal army at the <b>Battle of the Ghaghra</b> in 1529, and Sher Khan Sur defeated the sultan's forces at Surajgarh in 1534 and twice besieged Gaur, taking it in 1538 despite Portuguese help to…</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
